---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigFederationModels from '../figures/FigFederationModels.astro';
import FigTwoPaths from '../figures/FigTwoPaths.astro';
---

<Chapter id="landscape" num="02" kicker="Where federation stands" title="Trust already crosses domains, along paths nobody chose.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A PKI binds a public key to an identity, and trust in that binding climbs a chain of
      certificates up to an anchor the verifier has chosen. Inside one organisation this is solved.
      The difficulty begins when <strong>sovereign domains, each with its own policies and its own
      cryptography, need to accept each other's identities</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four models do it today. Two of them link the domains with certificates: a mesh of bilateral
      cross-certificates, or a bridge CA in the middle. The other two link them with a list or an
      agreement, and bound nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={1} caption="The four federation models, after the World Bank taxonomy (Tullis and Black, 2024).">
    <FigFederationModels />
  </Figure>

  <div class="prose after-fig">
    <p>
      Once several domains cross-certify, the hierarchy becomes a directed graph, and one credential
      can have more than one valid path. The Federal PKI playbook shows the same certificate
      validating through three CAs or through six, crossing two bridges, <strong>depending only on
      which anchor is installed</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={2} caption="One credential, two valid paths. Rebuilt from the Federal PKI path discovery and validation playbook (GSA).">
    <FigTwoPaths />
  </Figure>

  <p class="key">If there are several paths, someone has to choose among them.</p>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .after-fig {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
</style>
